      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by simplifying the given equation. The equation given is:\n\n\\[ 5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x+2} \\]\n\nFirst, let's define \\( f(x) \\) as the function on the right-hand side of the equation:\n\\[ f(x) = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x+2} \\]\n\nWe will find the roots of the numerator \\( x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x \\), because if \\( x \\) is a root of this numerator, it will also be a root of the original equation.\n\nFactor the numerator:\n\\[ x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x = x(x^2 - 2x - 8) \\]\n\\[ x^2 - 2x - 8 = (x-4)(x+2) \\]\n\nSo, the expression becomes:\n\\[ f(x) = \\frac{x(x-4)(x+2)}{x+2} \\]\n\nWe can cancel the \\( x+2 \\) term from the numerator and the denominator (for \\( x \\neq -2 \\)):\n\\[ f(x) = x(x-4) \\]\n\nThe original equation simplifies to:\n\\[ 5 = f(x) \\]\n\\[ 5 = x(x-4) \\]\n\nRewriting this, we get a quadratic equation:\n\\[ x^2 - 4x - 5 = 0 \\]\n\nWe can factor this quadratic:\n\\[ x^2 - 4x - 5 = (x-5)(x+1) = 0 \\]\n\nSetting each factor equal to zero, we find the roots:\n\\[ x - 5 = 0 \\implies x = 5 \\]\n\\[ x + 1 = 0 \\implies x = -1 \\]\n\nThus, the solutions to the equation are \\( x = 5 \\) and \\( x = -1 \\).\n\nThe sum of these solutions is:\n\\[ 5 + (-1) = 4 \\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the \\( x \\)-values that satisfy the original equation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]",
