      "text": "To solve the equation \\( 5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x+2} \\), we first rewrite it as a polynomial equation by clearing the denominator:\n\n\\[\n5(x + 2) = x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\n\\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying:\n\n\\[\n5x + 10 = x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\n\\]\n\nNext, we move all terms to one side to set the equation to zero:\n\n\\[\nx^3 - 2x^2 - 8x - 5x - 10 = 0\n\\]\n\nCombining like terms:\n\n\\[\nx^3 - 2x^2 - 13x - 10 = 0\n\\]\n\nTo find the \\( x \\)-values that satisfy this cubic equation, we use the Rational Root Theorem to identify possible rational roots. According to the theorem, possible rational roots are \\( \\pm 1, \\pm 10 \\) divided by \\( \\pm 1 \\).\n\nWe test \\( x = 1 \\):\n\n\\[\n1^3 - 2(1)^2 - 13(1) - 10 = 1 - 2 - 13 - 10 = -24 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n\\]\n\nNext, we test \\( x = -1 \\):\n\n\\[\n(-1)^3 - 2(-1)^2 - 13(-1) - 10 = -1 - 2 + 13 - 10 = 0 \\quad \\text{(a root)}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( x = -1 \\) is a root, we can factor \\( x + 1 \\) out of \\( x^3 - 2x^2 - 13x - 10 \\). Using polynomial division (or synthetic division), we divide \\( x^3 - 2x^2 - 13x - 10 \\) by \\( x + 1 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\left( x^3 - 2x^2 - 13x - 10 \\right) \\div (x + 1)\n\\]\n\nDividing results in:\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 3x - 10\n\\]\n\nNow, solve \\( x^2 - 3x - 10 = 0 \\) using the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -3 \\), and \\( c = -10 \\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{(-3)^2 - 4(1)(-10)}}{2(1)}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 + 40}}{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{49}}{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{3 \\pm 7}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two roots:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{3 + 7}{2} = 5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = \\frac{3 - 7}{2} = -2\n\\]\n\nThus, the \\( x \\)-values that satisfy the original equation are \\( x = -5, 5, \\) and \\( x = 2 \\). Adding these values together, we get:\n\n\\[\n-5 + 5 + 2 = 2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
