      "text": "To solve the equation \\(5=\\frac{x^3-2x^2-8x}{x+2}\\), we first need to simplify the right side of the equation.\n\nWe start by factoring \\(x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\\). Notice that \\(x\\) is a common factor:\n\\[\nx^3 - 2x^2 - 8x = x(x^2 - 2x - 8).\n\\]\nNext, we factor the quadratic expression \\(x^2 - 2x - 8\\). We look for two numbers that multiply to \\(-8\\) and add to \\(-2\\). These numbers are \\(-4\\) and \\(2\\). Thus,\n\\[\nx^2 - 2x - 8 = (x - 4)(x + 2).\n\\]\nSo, the original expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n5 = \\frac{x(x - 4)(x + 2)}{x + 2}.\n\\]\nFor \\(x \\neq -2\\), we can cancel the \\(x + 2\\) term in the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[\n5 = x(x - 4).\n\\]\nNow, we have a simpler quadratic equation to solve:\n\\[\n5 = x^2 - 4x.\n\\]\nRearranging all terms to one side of the equation, we get:\n\\[\nx^2 - 4x - 5 = 0.\n\\]\nWe can factor this quadratic equation as follows:\n\\[\nx^2 - 4x - 5 = (x - 5)(x + 1) = 0.\n\\]\nSetting each factor equal to zero gives us the solutions:\n\\[\nx = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -1.\n\\]\nWe must check if these solutions are valid by ensuring they do not make the original denominator zero. The original denominator is \\(x + 2\\), and neither \\(5\\) nor \\(-1\\) make this zero. Therefore, both solutions are valid.\n\nThe sum of the solutions is:\n\\[\n5 + (-1) = 4.\n\\]\nThus, the sum of the \\(x\\)-values that satisfy the equation is\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}.\n\\]",
